{"tool_name":"whatsgnu","contents":["biocontainers","oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biocontainers":{"name":"whatsgnu","identifiers":["doi:10.1186/s13059-020-01965-w"],"license":"GNU General Public License v3.0","summary":"Whatsgnu a tool for identifying proteomic novelty","home_url":"https://github.com/ahmedmagds/WhatsGNU","keywords":["Proteomics","Genetics","Protein Sequences","Ortholog"],"manually_check":true,"total_pulls":4},"biotools":{"id":"whatsgnu","home":"https://github.com/ahmedmagds/WhatsGNU","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"a tool for identifying proteomic novelty.\n\nWhatsGNU utilizes the natural variation in public databases to rank protein sequences based on the number of observed exact protein matches (the GNU score) in all known genomes of a certain species & can quickly create whole protein reports.","addition_date":"2021-01-18T08:08:52Z","last_update_date":"2021-03-14T11:50:41Z","biotools_curie":"biotools:whatsgnu","credit":[{"email":"planetp@email.chop.edu","name":"Paul J. Planet","typeEntity":"Person"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Multilocus sequence typing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3840"},{"term":"Query and retrieval","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0224"}]}],"name":"WhatsGNU","owner":"zsmag19","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/S13059-020-01965-W","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2020 The Author(s).To understand diversity in enormous collections of genome sequences, we need computationally scalable tools that can quickly contextualize individual genomes based on their similarities and identify features of each genome that make them unique. We present WhatsGNU, a tool based on exact match proteomic compression that, in seconds, classifies any new genome and provides a detailed report of protein alleles that may have novel functional differences. We use this technique to characterize the total allelic diversity (panallelome) of Salmonella enterica, Mycobacterium tuberculosis, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Staphylococcus aureus. It could be extended to others. WhatsGNU is available from https://github.com/ahmedmagds/WhatsGNU.","authors":[{"name":"Moustafa A.M."},{"name":"Planet P.J."}],"citationCount":2,"date":"2020-03-05T00:00:00Z","journal":"Genome Biology","title":"WhatsGNU: A tool for identifying proteomic novelty"},"pmcid":"PMC7059281","pmid":"32138767"}],"topic":[{"term":"Genetic variation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0199"},{"term":"Proteomics","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0121"},{"term":"Sequence analysis","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0080"}]},"bioschemas":{"name":"WhatsGNU","home":"https://bio.tools/whatsgnu","license":"GPL-3.0","summary":"a tool for identifying proteomic novelty.\n\nWhatsGNU utilizes the natural variation in public databases to rank protein sequences based on the number of observed exact protein matches (the GNU score) in all known genomes of a certain species & can quickly create whole protein reports.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:whatsgnu/undefined/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":true,"sourcecode":{"free":true,"interpreted":true,"license":true,"public":true,"readme":true,"repository":{"anonymous":true,"online":true}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":16,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2024"},{"count":2,"year":"2023"},{"count":3,"year":"2020"},{"count":3,"year":"2025"},{"count":3,"year":"2026"},{"count":4,"year":"2021"}],"doi":"10.1186/s13059-020-01965-w","pmcid":"PMC7059281","pmid":"32138767","ref_count":19,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"2002"},{"count":1,"year":"2008"},{"count":1,"year":"2009"},{"count":1,"year":"2010"},